# How this project is built

Every piece of work goes from a GitHub issue to a merged pull request through the same steps. AI
agents do most of the writing, testing and reviewing, and the owner decides at fixed points. This
page explains each step, who does it, and how it maps to the roles of a usual software team. The
rules themselves are in [`AGENTS.md`](../AGENTS.md) and the skills under
[`.agents/skills/`](../.agents/skills/); the process comes from the template named in
`.copier-answers.yml`.

## The steps, start to finish

| # | Step | What happens | Who does it | Usual role | Gate |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Product definition | What the product must do, and in which milestone | Owner, with the manager session | Product owner, business analyst | **Owner** |
| 2 | Backlog | A GitHub issue with a rating (red, yellow, green or chore), "blocked by" links, and the spec capabilities and ADR number it will take | Manager session | Project manager | **Owner:** what comes next |
| 3 | Design, point by point | For each point: what it means, the alternatives with merits and drawbacks, one recommendation, mockups for any screen. The agreed design goes into the issue | Manager proposes, owner decides | Business analyst, architect, UX designer | **Owner:** each point |
| 4 | Start the build | A session in its own worktree and branch from `main`, in a mode: autopilot, build or learning | Manager session, or a developer | Tech lead | **Owner:** the mode |
| 5 | Planning (OpenSpec) | Proposal (why, what), specs with Gherkin scenarios that run as acceptance tests, design and ADRs (how), tasks | Build session | BA, test lead, architect, tech lead | **Owner:** proposal, specs, ADRs. **Developer:** design, tasks |
| 6 | Draft pull request | One PR per change, opened when the planning is approved | Build session | Tech lead | — |
| 7 | Build (outside-in TDD) | Failing acceptance scenarios first, then unit tests and code until they pass: red, green, refactor | `senior-qa`, `senior-dev`, or the developer | QA automation, developer | — in autopilot |
| 8 | Continuous integration | Lint, types, unit and acceptance tests, spec validation, dependency audit, at two or three agreed push points | CI | DevOps | — |
| 9 | Verification | For UI: an independent verifier checks the real pages at {{ ui_verifier_width }} px against the issue's checklist. A code review by a different model covers security, permissions, data, server logic. Findings sorted into three levels; at most three rounds | Verifier, reviewer | QA tester, peer and security reviewer | Adversarial review only if the owner agrees |
| 10 | Demo | Review videos of the working flow, in journey order | Build session | Sprint review | **Owner:** watches them |
| 11 | Close the specs | `openspec verify`, then archive: the change's specs join `openspec/specs/`, the living description of the product | Build session | Technical writer | — |
| 12 | Merge | Checked against the issue point by point, then merged with a merge commit | Owner | Release manager | **Owner** |
| 13 | Learning | Lessons go back into the template, so every project gains them | Everyone | Retrospective | — |

## Who decides what

| Decision | Who | Recorded in |
|---|---|---|
| Scope, what comes next, milestones | Owner | The issue and its rating |
| The design: behaviour, screens, security choices | Owner, point by point | The issue's agreed design |
| The proposal and specs: what the change does | Owner | The change's first commits |
| A lasting technical choice: a new dependency, pattern or data layout | Developer proposes, owner decides | An ADR |
| The technical design inside the agreed design | The developer running the build | `design.md`, `tasks.md` |
| Code, tests, refactoring | The developer and their agent | Commits |
| The merge | Owner | A merge commit |

A product question the agreed design does not answer goes to the owner as a comment on the issue,
never decided in code. Its answer is added to the issue's design, so the next agent sees it.

## Modes

| Mode | The build stops for you… | Use it when |
|---|---|---|
| Autopilot | at the planning approval and at the end | The design is settled and you want speed |
| Build | at each step: every planning artifact, each test | You want to steer as it goes |
| Learning | at each step and before every command, explaining it | You are new to the code or the method |

## Review findings

| Level | Meaning | What happens |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Breaks core functionality: wrong results, data loss, a security hole | Fixed now |
| 2 | Plausible in real use, limited impact | Fixed if cheap, otherwise one issue for all of them |
| 3 | Rare or theoretical | One line in the PR under "Deliberately not handled" |

## A developer's change, step by step

1. Pick a ready issue: open, nobody assigned, no open blocker, with an agreed design. Start small.
2. Assign it to yourself, and make its worktree from `main`, with its own ports and database.
3. Start your agent in the worktree: "work on issue <n> in learning mode" while you are new to the
   code, build mode after that.
4. Your agent drafts the proposal and specs; ask the owner on the PR to approve them. You approve
   the design and tasks, unless they need an ADR.
5. Approve each batch of tests and code. Technical questions are yours; product questions go to
   the owner on the issue.
6. Run the same checks as CI, then the review. UI work also takes the independent verifier and
   the review videos.
7. Archive the change, then ask the owner to merge.

## Which SDLC this is

- **Agile, closest to Kanban:** work is pulled one issue at a time per worker, with no fixed
  sprints; milestones act as release goals.
- **Each issue is a small V-model:** requirements and scenarios are proved by acceptance tests;
  the design by review and the verifier; the code by unit tests, types and mutation testing.
- **Spec-driven, with BDD and TDD:** the spec is the source of truth, its scenarios run as tests,
  and tests are written before code.
- **Rules checked by machines where they can be:** each project convention names the check that
  enforces it, so reviews spend their time on what no check can see.

## Glossary

| Term | Meaning |
|---|---|
| Issue rating | Red: blocks other work. Yellow: needed soon. Green: can wait. Chore: upkeep with no feature. |
| Worktree | A second working folder of the same repository, on its own branch. |
| OpenSpec change | One unit of work: proposal, specs, design, tasks; then verify and archive. One issue, one PR. |
| Delta spec | The part of a spec a change adds or modifies; merged into the living specs at archive. |
| Gherkin | Given / When / Then scenarios in plain words, which also run as tests. |
| ADR | Architecture decision record: one decision, its alternatives and its consequences. |
| Outside-in TDD | Start from a failing acceptance test of what the user sees, then work inward. |
| Manager session | The agent session on `main` that designs with the owner, files issues and starts builds. |
| Build session | The agent session that carries one issue from planning to PR in its own worktree. |
| Subagent | A helper a build session starts for one job (`senior-qa`, `senior-dev`, the verifier). |
| Independent verifier | An agent that never saw the code, checking the real pages against the issue's checklist. |
